<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Worker">

  <!--
    ===================================================================
    NOT EVERY MICROSERVICE IS A WEB API.
    ===================================================================

    Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Worker, not Sdk.Web. There is no Kestrel here, no port,
    no Swagger, no controller, no launchSettings applicationUrl - and there is
    no gateway route to it, because there is nothing to route TO.

    The temptation, when every other service in a solution looks the same, is
    to give this one a Web SDK and a /health endpoint "for consistency". Resist
    it. Architecture should reflect responsibility: this service's job is to
    react to things that happened elsewhere, and reacting needs no inbox on
    port 7107.

    It is also the clearest possible demonstration of what a message broker
    buys you. Ordering does not call Notification. Ordering does not know
    Notification exists - there is no client, no base URL, no timeout, no
    retry policy, and nothing to configure when a second consumer is added
    next month. Compare that with the Basket and Inventory clients in
    Ordering.Infrastructure, each of which had to be written, configured,
    timed out and failure-handled by hand.

    Stop this worker and orders still complete; the messages wait in its queue.
    Stop Inventory in Phase 8 and orders failed with a 503. That difference is
    the whole argument for asynchronous messaging, and you can demonstrate it
    in thirty seconds.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <UserSecretsId>32c28cee-8f4b-4a0d-b684-8d7883a0d344</UserSecretsId>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\..\BuildingBlocks\CommerceFlow.BuildingBlocks.Messaging\CommerceFlow.BuildingBlocks.Messaging.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting" Version="10.0.12" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
